    <h2>Piece Count Is Marketing, Not Coverage</h2>

    <p>Here is the thing nobody tells you when you are staring at two boxes on a shelf. High piece counts are reached with filler. Bits, hex keys, a spinner handle, an adapter, an extension in every length, and 12-point duplicates of sizes you already own in 6-point.</p>

    <p>You will use perhaps 20 sockets out of either set with real regularity. The question is whether those 20 are in there and whether they skip.</p>

    <p>GEARWRENCH's reputation rests on the no-skipped-sizes claim, and it is the reason mechanics recommend the brand. A set that jumps 13mm to 15mm is worse than a smaller set that has both, because the one time you need the missing size you drive to a store.</p>

    <h2>What the DEWALT Actually Buys You</h2>

    <p>Two real things, and one of them is worth money. The lifetime warranty is the easy one: break a socket, walk in, walk out with a new one. Over ten years that is a genuine advantage.</p>

    <p>The second is the hard shell case. A blow-mold case that flexes will dump sockets in your trunk eventually. Every mechanic has lost a 10mm this way.</p>

    <p>What the DEWALT does not buy you is fundamentally different steel. Both are chrome vanadium-class hand-tool steel, and neither is impact rated. Neither one goes on an impact wrench, ever.</p>

    <h2>The Rule That Matters More Than Either Set</h2>

    <p>A chrome socket on an impact wrench is how you learn what shrapnel looks like. Chrome vanadium is hard, and hard means it shatters. Impact sockets are made from a softer, thicker alloy specifically so they deform instead of exploding.</p>

    <p>If you own a cordless impact, budget for a separate impact socket set and keep the two collections apart. The <a href="/posts/best-impact-socket-set-review" style="color:var(--accent);">impact socket set review</a> covers that side, and the <a href="/tools/impact-socket-set" style="color:var(--accent);">impact socket comparison</a> lists the options.</p>

    <p>Also worth knowing: 6-point sockets grip the flats of a bolt head. 12-point sockets let you work in a tight swing arc but grip the corners and are likelier to round a rusty nut. Reach for 6-point on anything stubborn.</p>

    <p>One honest thing: the cheapest way into sockets is not a 192-piece box. A single 3/8-inch set covering 8mm to 19mm in 6-point handles the majority of what a home mechanic does on a modern car. Buy that first, add sizes as you hit them, and you will spend less than either set on this page and own fewer sockets you never touch.</p>

    <h2>Verdict</h2>

    <p><strong>Buy the GEARWRENCH 110PC</strong> if you want quality per piece and no missing sizes in the range you actually work in.</p>

    <p><strong>Buy the DEWALT 192PC</strong> if you value the lifetime warranty and the case, and you would rather have too much than drive out for one 15mm.</p>

    <p>Either set pairs with a <a href="/tools/ratchet-set" style="color:var(--accent);">ratchet set</a> and a <a href="/tools/breaker-bar" style="color:var(--accent);">breaker bar</a> for the bolts that will not move, and a <a href="/posts/best-socket-set-review" style="color:var(--accent);">full socket set review</a> if you want to see the $54 budget option and the impact-rated Sunex master set side by side. For the sizes that round off no matter what you use, the <a href="/tools/bolt-extractor-set" style="color:var(--accent);">bolt extractor set</a> is the next stop.</p>
